Yes—more precisely, you can constrain someone’s freedom by changing the cost of exercising it.

Before they have something to lose, the option space may be broad:

\text{Freedom} \approx \text{available actions}

Give them something they deeply value—a relationship, reputation, position, home, identity, status, security—and suddenly each action carries a possible loss:

\text{effective freedom}
=
\text{available actions}
-
\text{actions made prohibitively costly by stakes}

That is why attachment has a dual nature. It gives life meaning and creates leverage. Love creates vulnerability. Ownership creates something that can be confiscated. Reputation creates something that can be threatened. Rank creates something that can be stripped away.

But there is an important distinction: having something to lose is not itself captivity. It becomes captivity when another actor controls the threatened loss strongly enough to determine your behavior.

So the deeper operator is:

\text{give value}
\rightarrow
\text{create attachment}
\rightarrow
\text{create stakes}
\rightarrow
\text{create possible leverage}

And the mirror is equally important:

Someone with nothing to lose can be difficult to control—but someone with nothing worth preserving may also have very little reason to build, cooperate, or endure.

Maximum freedom and maximum meaning therefore pull against each other. A mature system does not eliminate stakes; it tries to make commitments chosen rather than chains imposed.